Yes. There’s a coherent structure in that.

A lot of adult male play gets rebranded so it can survive status pressure. “Pretend” becomes training, simulation, sport, collecting, fantasy football, reenactment, gaming, tactical planning, world-building, entrepreneurship, military exercises, even investing. The imaginative machinery is still there; the culture often just gives it a more acceptable costume.

That matters because imagination is not decorative. It is a counterfactual engine:

current state → imagined alternative → simulated consequence → possible action

Kill the capacity for play and you can also blunt experimentation, empathy, rehearsal, invention, and the ability to imagine a life that does not yet exist.

Your future-self move is a particularly useful example. You do not need literal retrocausality for it to work. You can borrow reward from an anticipated future:

future self I value
→ simulate his perspective
→ reinterpret present effort
→ effort feels meaningful now

The future has not sent validation backward through time; your present nervous system is simulating the future strongly enough that its anticipated meaning changes present motivation. That is already a remarkable capability.

Likewise with pain and work. The sensation can remain unpleasant while its meaning changes:

pain = pointless suffering

versus

pain = evidence of chosen exertion toward something I value

Same signal, different interpretation, different behavioral response. That can be useful, but it is worth retaining the ability to tell productive discomfort from actual damage. A good spell should still leave the smoke detector connected.

And I think your final point is the most important: removing a cage is not sufficient if the cage was also supplying orientation.

If somebody leaves a life organized entirely around work, doctrine, institution, relationship, military structure, or some other rigid system, suddenly giving them unlimited possibility can be terrifying. They may return to the old structure not because it was good, but because it supplied:

map
role
rhythm
community
feedback
next action

So liberation needs infrastructure:

map: here are the possible worlds.
compass: here are the values you chose.
keys: here are the capabilities and resources available to you.
base camp: here is somewhere safe to return while experimenting.
playground: here is somewhere failure is inexpensive.

Then freedom stops meaning “figure everything out alone.”

It becomes increasing degrees of freedom with increasing navigational ability.

That also connects back to LARPing. Play is one of nature’s cheapest ways of learning a world before consequences become expensive. Young animals rehearse pursuit, fighting, escape, social boundaries, courtship, and coordination through play. Humans can do something even stranger: we can rehearse worlds that do not exist yet.

So maybe the price of looking permanently cool is too high if the payment is your ability to imagine.

A better bargain is to become secure enough that occasionally looking ridiculous no longer threatens who you think you are.

Then the man gets to keep the child’s laboratory without having to become the child again.